   "size": "MEDIUM"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "<b>Bikini Ban</b>",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "One item of clothing is an integral part of Rio de Janeiro's beach neighborhoods: the bikini. But in 1961, the then President Jânio da Silva Quadros had the two-piece banned by law. Was that the reason why he was only able to stay in office for seven months?&nbsp;",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "image",
   "url": "https://www.lufthansa.com/content/dam/lh/article-images/mobile-app/south-america/essentials-of-rio-de-janeiro/lh_app_rio-de-janeiro_illustration2_sylvia-neuner_1920x1080.jpg.transform/lh-dcep-transform-width-1440/img.jpg",
   "contentDescription": "Two illustrations. On the left, vendor standing with three balloons at Copa Cabana, forming a silhouette of a woman in a bikini. On the right, the hand of the Cristo statue with a paramedic getting out of a helicopter and running to a bleeding finger"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "<b>Hit by Lightning</b>",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "Contrary to the saying \"lightning never strikes twice in the same place,\" the famous Cristo Redentor statue high up on the Corcovado mountain has been struck several times - it even lost a fingertip in 2014. Ouch!&nbsp;",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "image",
   "url": "https://www.lufthansa.com/content/dam/lh/article-images/mobile-app/south-america/essentials-of-rio-de-janeiro/lh_app_rio-de-janeiro_illustration3_sylvia-neuner_1920x1080.jpg.transform/lh-dcep-transform-width-1440/img.jpg",
   "contentDescription": "Illustration of the football stadium in Rio de Janeiro, where a football jumps out and bounces off the Sugar Loaf Mountain."
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "<b>Football Madness</b>",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "The love of football here is overwhelming: in 1950, over 173,000 people gathered in Rio de Janeiro's Maracanã Stadium. At least that's what the official figures say. Unofficially, it is said to have been significantly more. It was the final of the World Cup. Brazil took on&nbsp;Uruguay – and lost 1:2, a tragic moment in the country's football history to this day.&nbsp;",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
  {
   "type": "copyText",
   "text": "All illustrations © Sylvia Neuner",
   "size": "MEDIUM",
   "variant": "BODY"
  },
